== where the ## split happened and the frozen line that fixes it ==
The frozen SourceRule.makeUpRule splits the rule on the literal "##":
    val ruleStrS = rule.split("##")            // AnalyzeRule.kt:686 (pinned 14dd249)
Kotlin's split takes its default limit of zero (no limit), so it keeps the trailing empty field:
"#meta@text##忘语##忘语先生##" has four fields, and lines 694-695 set replaceFirst = true when
ruleStrS.size > 3. The replaceFirst branch (AnalyzeRule.kt:426-437) answers with the replaced
*matched* text, not the whole value, so the handset returns "忘语先生" for that rule.
The adapter split the same rule the Java way (dropping the trailing empty field, so three fields
and replace-all) in packages/fjs/liber_html/src/rule.rs::split_replace and in the shared field
parser lib/source/rule_field.dart::splitRuleFields; #51 removed both trailing-empty trims. The
corpus expectation for replacement-trailing-field was corrected from "作者：忘语先生" to "忘语先生"
in the same fix (the old value was read from a Java-style split and was wrong).

